## 2. Problem Definition
The Net Zero Commission’s 2026 Annual Progress Report indicates that New South Wales is falling short of its 2030 target to cut emissions by 50% from 2005 levels—projecting an actual reduction of only 44%, leaving a gap of 10 to 20 million tonnes of $\text{CO}_2\text{-e}$[cite: 1]. Closing this gap requires both technological innovation in localized power generation and strategic policy interventions in hard-to-abate sectors like agriculture[cite: 1, 2].

### Performance Requirements
* **Must-Have Constraints:**
  * Achieve reliable self-starting rotation at low airspeeds ($2.0 - 3.5\text{ m/s}$).
  * Generate measurable electrical output ($V \times I$) on a standard multimeter rig.
  * Retain structural integrity under rotational shear without layer separation.
  * Use a fully parametric CAD pipeline to enable rapid geometry adjustments[cite: 2].
* **Nice-to-Have Goals:**
  * Achieve an effective Tip-Speed Ratio ($\text{TSR}$) approaching lift-based operating regimes ($\text{TSR} \approx 3 - 6$)[cite: 2].
  * Maintain a narrow trailing edge ($0.7\text{ mm}$) without causing 3D printing defects[cite: 2].

