## 3. Background Research
Wind turbine power extraction is governed by the wind power equation:

$$P = \frac{1}{2} \rho A v^3 C_p$$

Where:
* $P$ = Power output in Watts[cite: 2]
* $\rho$ = Air density ($\approx 1.225\text{ kg/m}^3$)[cite: 2]
* $A$ = Swept area of the rotor blades ($m^2$)[cite: 2]
* $v$ = Oncoming wind speed ($m/s$)[cite: 2]
* $C_p$ = Power coefficient / conversion efficiency[cite: 2]

According to Betz's Law, no turbine can capture more than $59.3\%$ ($16/27$) of the kinetic energy in wind[cite: 2]. The remaining energy must stay in the airflow so it can move away from the turbine[cite: 2].

                 BETZ'S LAW LIMIT (59.3% MAX EFFICIENCY)
                                   |
       +---------------------------+---------------------------+
       |                                                       |
High Solidity (4-6 Blades)                              Low Solidity (3 Blades)

Higher starting torque                                - Reduced rotational drag

Increased drag at speed                               - Higher peak Tip-Speed Ratio

Favored for low-wind startup                          - Optimal lift-to-drag balance


Because small-scale rotors operate at low Reynolds numbers ($Re$), airflow over the blade is prone to separation, causing stall and high pressure drag[cite: 2]:

$$Re = \frac{\rho v L}{\mu}$$

Where $L$ is the blade chord length and $\mu$ is dynamic viscosity[cite: 2]. Utilizing an aerofoil tailored for low $Re$, such as the **Eppler e387**, maintains laminar flow over a wider range of operating conditions[cite: 2].

