## 6. Engineering Decisions
* **3-Blade Rotor Configuration:** Selected a 3-blade layout to balance high starting torque with low rotational drag, matching commercial horizontal-axis standards[cite: 2].
* **Eppler e387 Aerofoil Selection:** Chosen specifically for its ability to maintain smooth laminar flow at low Reynolds numbers, reducing boundary layer separation at low airspeeds[cite: 2].
* **$0.7\text{ mm}$ Trailing Edge:** Instead of a theoretical $0.1\text{ mm}$ edge, I set the trailing edge to $0.7\text{ mm}$[cite: 2]. This preserved print quality on FDM equipment while preventing blunt-edge pressure drag[cite: 2].
* **Vertical Print Alignment:** Printed blades vertically on the build plate[cite: 2]. While this placed layer lines perpendicular to centrifugal forces, it yielded a much smoother aerofoil finish than horizontal printing, significantly reducing surface drag[cite: 2].

Vertical Alignment (Selected)             Horizontal Alignment (Rejected)
|                                         /-------------------

| Smooth profile surface                 /  Stepped layer lines

| Reduced aerodynamic drag              /   High surface friction


## 7. Technical Explanation

### Tip-Speed Ratio ($\text{TSR}$)
* **Simple Explanation:** Tip-Speed Ratio compares how fast the tip of a turbine blade is moving relative to the speed of the wind blowing against it[cite: 2].
* **Analogy:** Imagine running with a pinwheel. If you run fast but the wheel spins slowly, it has a low tip-speed ratio. If a light breeze makes the wheel spin into a fast blur, it has a high tip-speed ratio.
* **Technical Explanation:** $\text{TSR}$ ($\lambda$) is expressed as:
  $$\lambda = \frac{\omega R}{v}$$
  Where $\omega$ is rotational velocity in radians/sec, $R$ is rotor radius, and $v$ is wind velocity[cite: 2]. Lift-based horizontal-axis wind turbines operate most efficiently at a $\text{TSR}$ between 3 and 8[cite: 2].
* **Application:** The tested prototypes operated at a $\text{TSR}$ of approximately $0.3 - 0.5$[cite: 2]. This showed that the rotor was driven primarily by drag rather than aerodynamic lift, identifying a key area for geometry improvements in future iterations[cite: 2].

## 8. Calculations and Engineering Analysis
I calculated the operational Tip-Speed Ratio ($\lambda$) using experimental performance data[cite: 2].

**Variables:**
* Rotational speed ($N$) = $49\text{ RPM}$ (Version D1 under load)[cite: 2]
* Rotor Radius ($R$) = $0.15\text{ m}$
* Airspeed ($v$) = $3.5\text{ m/s}$[cite: 2]

**Step 1: Calculate angular velocity ($\omega$)**
$$\omega = \frac{N \times 2\pi}{60} = \frac{49 \times 2\pi}{60} \approx 5.131\text{ rad/s}$$

**Step 2: Calculate tip velocity ($v_{\text{tip}}$)**
$$v_{\text{tip}} = \omega \times R = 5.131 \times 0.15 \approx 0.770\text{ m/s}$$

**Step 3: Calculate Tip-Speed Ratio ($\lambda$)**
$$\lambda = \frac{v_{\text{tip}}}{v} = \frac{0.770}{3.5} \approx 0.220$$

**Engineering Significance:**
The resulting $\text{TSR}$ ($\approx 0.22 - 0.5$) fell below the target lift-based range ($3 - 6$)[cite: 2]. This confirmed that while widening the root chord provided the torque needed to spin the rotor under load, it also introduced rotational drag that capped peak efficiency[cite: 2].

