## 14. Reflection
I originally assumed that achieving a high free-running (unloaded) RPM was the primary indicator of a successful blade design. Testing Version B1 proved this wrong: while it reached $63\text{ RPM}$ unloaded, its speed dropped to $30\text{ RPM}$ as soon as a generator load was applied[cite: 2]. Increasing profile thickness (Version C1) reduced top speed slightly but vastly improved torque retention under load[cite: 2].

Additionally, completing this project showed me that engineering technical solutions is only half the battle. To drive actual emissions reductions, technology must be paired with effective policy[cite: 1]. Formulating a formal submission to the NSW Minister for Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Heritage taught me how to translate experimental findings into scalable legislative proposals[cite: 1].

